1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Thousands of years of hidden history sit beneath its streets.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:11,000
Peel back the layers of time and find some of the technological wonders of the ancient world.

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:17,000
A grand palace, an enormous stadium, and an ingenious system for storing water,

4
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:21,000
built by emperors and buried by their conquerors.

5
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:27,000
Now, uncover one of the lost cities of the underworld, Istanbul, on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:43,000
It's been called Byzantium, New Rome, Constantinople.

7
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:48,160
This strategic peninsula sits with the Marmara Sea on one side and the Bosphorus Strait on the other.

8
00:00:48,760 --> 00:00:51,960
It's the only city in the world that straddles two continents.

9
00:00:52,340 --> 00:00:58,660
This prime location made it the perfect spot for empires to rise, fall, and rise again.

10
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,040
I'm Eric Geller, I'm in Istanbul, Turkey, and for centuries this was the most powerful

11
00:01:07,041 --> 00:01:08,040
city in the world.

12
00:01:08,041 --> 00:01:13,280
But when you're on top, the only place to go is down, and down is where these empires

13
00:01:13,281 --> 00:01:14,280
went.

14
00:01:14,281 --> 00:01:17,280
If you dig anywhere you'll find evidence of the fallen civilizations.

15
00:01:17,281 --> 00:01:21,380
There are cisterns and libraries, dungeons, hidden tunnels.

16
00:01:21,381 --> 00:01:23,960
There are entire cities buried beneath our feet.

17
00:01:23,961 --> 00:01:28,200
Now we can't shut down today's modern day city to excavate, but there are other ways

18
00:01:28,201 --> 00:01:29,200
to get underground.

19
00:01:29,201 --> 00:01:37,580
Today, Istanbul is part of the Democratic Republic of modern Turkey, and the city's

20
00:01:37,581 --> 00:01:41,920
11 million inhabitants make up a melting pot of the cultures these fallen empires have

21
00:01:41,921 --> 00:01:42,920
left behind.

22
00:01:42,921 --> 00:01:47,920
Everywhere you look, the past and the present collide.

23
00:01:47,921 --> 00:01:52,660
Taxis whiz by the 1,500-year-old Byzantine church, Hagia Sophia.

24
00:01:52,661 --> 00:01:55,740
Couples stroll by 1,000-year-old Roman city walls.

25
00:01:55,741 --> 00:01:59,060
Tourists take pictures of the almost 400-year-old Ottoman Blue Mosque.

26
00:01:59,780 --> 00:02:02,440
In most cities around the world, this would be it.

27
00:02:02,780 --> 00:02:05,240
But in Istanbul, it's only the beginning.

28
00:02:06,300 --> 00:02:08,040
We'll peel back the layers of time,

29
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:11,440
whisk away today's houses, busy streets, and modern bazaars

30
00:02:11,441 --> 00:02:15,240
to find a hidden history like no other city in the world.

31
00:02:16,420 --> 00:02:19,380
I'm not talking about a catacomb or a tunnel or two.

32
00:02:20,380 --> 00:02:24,480
We're walking on top of entire cities, stacked like bricks.

33
00:02:24,481 --> 00:02:27,280
Each layer of history buried deeper than the next.

34
00:02:29,820 --> 00:02:32,100
The city began in the 7th century B.C.

35
00:02:32,101 --> 00:02:35,100
as a small Greek fishing village called Byzantium.

36
00:02:36,080 --> 00:02:39,700
200 years later, the village became a part of the vast Roman Empire.

37
00:02:40,340 --> 00:02:41,261
And in 330 A.D.

38
00:02:41,262 --> 00:02:46,540
it was renamed Constantinople after Emperor Constantine moved his entire capital east.

39
00:02:47,220 --> 00:02:50,760
The Eastern Roman Empire, later known as the Byzantine Empire, was born.

40
00:02:50,880 --> 00:02:54,080
For more than a thousand years, the Byzantines flourished here.

41
00:02:54,480 --> 00:03:01,480
Then in 1453, Constantinople was conquered by the Ottomans, becoming Istanbul.

42
00:03:01,481 --> 00:03:06,480
Almost 500 years after that, the modern Turkish Republic was born.

43
00:03:06,481 --> 00:03:13,480
For 27 consecutive centuries, these civilizations, the Greeks, the Romans and the Turks, built

44
00:03:13,481 --> 00:03:19,480
palaces, stadiums, bathhouses, roads, each one trying to outdo the one before.

45
00:03:19,481 --> 00:03:25,140
Each time the city was destroyed, the next generation simply rebuilt atop the ruins of the past,

46
00:03:25,700 --> 00:03:31,460
adding their own layer, and in some cases, raising the ground level by more than 40 feet.

47
00:03:32,680 --> 00:03:34,200
But how is this possible?

48
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:38,620
How does an Ottoman mosque literally sit on top of a lost Roman palace?

49
00:03:39,540 --> 00:03:43,040
Or an apartment building not cave into the ancient Roman cistern below?

50
00:03:43,041 --> 00:03:53,040
My quest for answers begins almost 1700 years ago, when Istanbul was the new capital of the

51
00:03:53,041 --> 00:03:54,040
Roman Empire.

52
00:03:54,041 --> 00:04:00,040
For centuries, this city's prime location made it the most desirable spot in the world.

53
00:04:00,041 --> 00:04:04,040
The Romans knew this and they acted quickly to defend their new capital.

54
00:04:04,041 --> 00:04:09,040
They built impenetrable city walls, they chained off their harbors, and perhaps most ingeniously,

55
00:04:09,041 --> 00:04:12,740
they found a way to protect their greatest resource, drinking water.

56
00:04:13,240 --> 00:04:14,660
And it all started right here.

57
00:04:18,840 --> 00:04:22,620
The aqueduct was one of the Roman Empire's greatest engineering feats.

58
00:04:23,220 --> 00:04:29,980
Beginning in the 4th century, Constantinople's water began its journey 12 miles from the city center in the forest of Belgrade.

59
00:04:30,660 --> 00:04:35,100
Powered entirely by gravity, the precise slope of the aqueduct's construction

60
00:04:35,101 --> 00:04:38,720
ingeniously carried large amounts of water across uneven ground.

61
00:04:39,040 --> 00:04:41,200
until it was safely inside the city walls.

62
00:04:42,960 --> 00:04:46,760
The water was then diverted to one of five open-air cisterns.

63
00:04:47,260 --> 00:04:49,000
Here, it would purify itself,

64
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:51,320
depositing mineral and organic matters

65
00:04:51,321 --> 00:04:52,920
while still keeping its pressure.

66
00:04:53,920 --> 00:04:57,100
Next, the water made its way into the many subterranean tunnels

67
00:04:57,101 --> 00:04:58,620
snaking beneath the city streets.

68
00:04:59,320 --> 00:05:03,260
Its final destination was one of the thousands of underground cisterns.

69
00:05:03,640 --> 00:05:06,440
Here, it was stored until a citizen of Constantinople

70
00:05:06,441 --> 00:05:08,980
decided to pump water from the fountain up above.

71
00:05:09,040 --> 00:05:18,940
I met up with historical guide Bora Sertbash in Sultanahmet Square.

72
00:05:19,200 --> 00:05:20,680
Hi, how are you?

73
00:05:21,020 --> 00:05:23,880
He was going to show me the largest underground cistern in the city.

74
00:05:24,260 --> 00:05:27,760
Many believed it was from here that the emperor himself got his water.

75
00:05:30,860 --> 00:05:34,320
In a minute, you won't believe your eyes what you will see down here.

76
00:05:34,680 --> 00:05:37,500
This is a totally different life than what you've seen above.

77
00:05:39,040 --> 00:05:46,040
It's called the Basilica Cistern, but the Turks call it Yarabatan Sarayı, or Sunken Palace.

78
00:05:46,041 --> 00:05:49,040
And that's exactly what it looked like.

79
00:05:52,040 --> 00:05:54,040
This is amazing. This is...

80
00:05:54,041 --> 00:05:56,040
Look at the size of this place.

81
00:05:56,041 --> 00:05:58,040
This is a huge place.

82
00:05:58,041 --> 00:06:01,040
You would have no idea coming in off of the square.

83
00:06:01,041 --> 00:06:02,040
Yeah.

84
00:06:02,041 --> 00:06:04,040
I mean, there's restaurants right there.

85
00:06:04,041 --> 00:06:05,040
About here.

86
00:06:05,041 --> 00:06:06,040
There's a park right here.

87
00:06:06,041 --> 00:06:07,040
Police station, everything.

88
00:06:07,041 --> 00:06:08,040
This is massive.

89
00:06:08,041 --> 00:06:13,340
And we are going to see about over 100,000 square feet of an area here.

90
00:06:13,740 --> 00:06:18,080
This is the biggest underground cistern ever built by the Romans in the city.

91
00:06:19,280 --> 00:06:23,300
Emperor Justinian was responsible for building this cistern in the 6th century.

92
00:06:23,640 --> 00:06:29,920
He also built the nearby Hagia Sophia and was the emperor under which paganism died and Christianity flourished.

93
00:06:29,921 --> 00:06:35,100
During his 43-year rule, he was ambitiously able to reclaim the Western Empire,

94
00:06:35,600 --> 00:06:38,980
reform Roman law, and rebuild much of Constantinople.

95
00:06:39,700 --> 00:06:43,800
Today, the Basilica Cistern is one of the largest Roman structures left in the world.

96
00:06:44,680 --> 00:06:48,680
It's 459 feet long and 229 feet wide.

97
00:06:49,320 --> 00:06:54,920
The roof is supported by 336 marble pillars spaced approximately 15 feet apart.

98
00:06:55,760 --> 00:06:58,980
The cistern was the key to the survival of the empire.

99
00:06:58,981 --> 00:07:04,320
Since the city was constantly under siege, the aqueducts, which acted as natural bridges

100
00:07:04,321 --> 00:07:07,840
into the city, had to be broken to prevent entry.

101
00:07:07,841 --> 00:07:11,800
The water stored in the cistern helped sustain the residents of Constantinople for months

102
00:07:11,801 --> 00:07:12,800
at a time.

103
00:07:12,801 --> 00:07:21,000
After the conquest 1453, when the Ottomans came into the city, nobody gave them a tour.

104
00:07:21,001 --> 00:07:23,820
So they didn't know where is what.

105
00:07:23,821 --> 00:07:28,960
For that reason, the underground systems were not discovered for a long time.

106
00:07:28,961 --> 00:07:32,920
But even after they were discovered, the Ottomans didn't feel as vulnerable to attack as the

107
00:07:32,921 --> 00:07:36,260
Byzantines, so they didn't rely on them as much.

108
00:07:36,261 --> 00:07:41,020
In addition, the Ottomans' cultural preference for running water, not still, helped add to

109
00:07:41,021 --> 00:07:42,020
the cistern's downfall.

110
00:07:42,021 --> 00:07:46,020
In the Turkish culture, still water is considered dirty.

111
00:07:46,021 --> 00:07:50,280
When it runs, it's clean water, and this is what we use.

112
00:07:50,281 --> 00:07:55,340
So the Basilica cistern was forgotten beneath the streets until 1544, when a Frenchman named

113
00:07:55,341 --> 00:08:00,860
pierre giles became suspicious of the endless supplies of water and fish the locals were

114
00:08:00,861 --> 00:08:05,740
pulling out of their basements giles is credited with rediscovering the basilica cistern after

115
00:08:05,741 --> 00:08:12,300
being invited to fish beneath the basement of an old ottoman home today the basilica cistern

116
00:08:12,301 --> 00:08:18,780
sits more than 25 feet underneath the modern city for almost three city blocks anything you see

117
00:08:18,781 --> 00:08:24,500
Trains, restaurants, hotels, even the police station is supported by this 6th century system.

118
00:08:27,300 --> 00:08:34,080
The decorative marble columns credited with holding up today's city were once immersed in water and were never meant to be seen.

119
00:08:34,900 --> 00:08:37,740
They were just the recycled trash of Byzantine construction.

120
00:08:38,480 --> 00:08:40,980
Leftovers taken from nearby temples or monuments.

121
00:08:41,820 --> 00:08:43,082
How come it doesn't fall down?

122
00:08:43,083 --> 00:08:45,480
How come, you know, the police station isn't right there?

123
00:08:45,860 --> 00:08:47,480
How come this whole city hasn't caved in?

124
00:08:47,481 --> 00:08:49,480
That's a good question.

125
00:08:49,481 --> 00:08:53,480
Now at this point, we should remember the very important saying.

126
00:08:53,481 --> 00:08:59,480
City is built on seven principal hills, like Rome.

127
00:08:59,481 --> 00:09:06,480
When they had a valley, they erect columns in there, put in the Roman arch, cover it.

128
00:09:06,481 --> 00:09:09,480
With one stone you shoot two birds.

129
00:09:09,481 --> 00:09:14,480
You can have a water storage underneath and you have a flat surface now above.

130
00:09:14,481 --> 00:09:19,480
Approximately 1500 years and 25 major earthquakes later,

131
00:09:19,481 --> 00:09:23,480
these columns were still supporting the cistern and the city above.

132
00:09:23,481 --> 00:09:27,480
But they were also meant to hide some of its secrets.

133
00:09:29,480 --> 00:09:30,480
So who is this fine person?

134
00:09:30,481 --> 00:09:32,480
This is Medusa from the Greek mythology.

135
00:09:32,481 --> 00:09:35,480
Why is Medusa here? Is there some symbolic reason for that?

136
00:09:35,481 --> 00:09:37,480
That's a very good question actually.

137
00:09:37,481 --> 00:09:41,480
One explanation is, since everything is recycled material here,

138
00:09:41,481 --> 00:09:45,226
This is what they needed. They needed this size of a stone.

139
00:09:45,227 --> 00:09:49,480
They put it underneath and they elevate it to the height they wish.

140
00:09:49,481 --> 00:09:53,480
But in Istanbul there are always other reasons.

141
00:09:53,481 --> 00:10:01,480
Another explanation is 532 is the year the most Christian emperor Justinian is building across the street the Hagia Sophia.

142
00:10:01,481 --> 00:10:05,480
The biggest church ever built in the Roman period.

143
00:10:05,481 --> 00:10:16,340
He was a very religious person, and by burying Medusa, a pagan symbol, under the column, on the side, and there upside down, drowning her inside the water,

144
00:10:16,700 --> 00:10:21,340
he was giving a message that the pagan believes is helpless even to itself.

145
00:10:21,420 --> 00:10:28,580
So not only are they storing water here, but they're hiding the past, they're burying paganism at the bottom of the cistern.

146
00:10:28,820 --> 00:10:29,760
I couldn't say it better.

147
00:10:29,761 --> 00:10:35,000
Istanbul has been called the city of cisterns and while the Basilica cistern is the biggest

148
00:10:35,001 --> 00:10:39,960
it's just one of the hundreds of gaping ancient holes forgotten beneath the modern city streets

149
00:10:39,961 --> 00:10:45,040
I would say even some people wouldn't know they have cisterns under their houses and

150
00:10:45,041 --> 00:10:48,840
they're sitting on a heritage of 1,500 years old without even knowing

151
00:10:48,841 --> 00:10:56,220
Nakash is located in the old quarter of the city and specializes in high-end Turkish carpets

152
00:10:56,221 --> 00:11:02,580
pottery and jewelry but their real treasure is hidden beneath the store owner Genghis Korkmaz

153
00:11:02,581 --> 00:11:08,220
agreed to take me down yeah you can definitely feel the humidity this is our system amazing

154
00:11:08,221 --> 00:11:15,720
amazing isn't that beautiful it's amazing a cistern of this size would normally require

155
00:11:15,721 --> 00:11:21,060
only about eight columns for support but this one has 18 indicating an important

156
00:11:21,061 --> 00:11:25,440
palace building might have been on top of it. Today, history is repeating itself.

