250
00:18:23,300 --> 00:18:26,540
So you can roughly see what would have been the outlines of the Hippodrome.

251
00:18:26,980 --> 00:18:31,740
From about that green fountain where the road follows around was this massive race course.

252
00:18:32,140 --> 00:18:35,460
Goes around the obelisks and right around that building all the way around here.

253
00:18:35,520 --> 00:18:38,740
You would have seen chariot races and all the festivities that went on in the Hippodrome.

254
00:18:38,980 --> 00:18:40,600
This must have been a massive place.

255
00:18:40,601 --> 00:18:47,600
But the transition from horses rounding the tracks to taxis 15 feet above didn't happen overnight.

256
00:18:47,601 --> 00:18:51,600
The tradition of the Hippodrome came from the pagan Greeks,

257
00:18:51,601 --> 00:18:54,600
and in a city full of both Christian and Muslim influences,

258
00:18:54,601 --> 00:18:58,600
it was no wonder it ended up buried beneath the ground.

259
00:18:58,601 --> 00:19:05,600
This Hippodrome, for 1,000 years, center, heart of the Roman Empire.

260
00:19:05,601 --> 00:19:19,109
It was important for Byzantines also, but the importance changed because in paganism you live in this earth, in this life, but in Christianity this is your suffering

261
00:19:19,110 --> 00:19:19,810
place.

262
00:19:19,811 --> 00:19:23,600
If you are going to be a good believer, you don't have time to joy for entertaining.

263
00:19:23,601 --> 00:19:30,600
The pious Byzantines may have had no time for fun at the Hippodrome, but it was another religious group who silenced the stands for good.

264
00:19:30,601 --> 00:19:31,301
for good.

265
00:19:31,302 --> 00:19:37,700
In 1204, the Fourth Crusades breached the city walls, and the richest Christian city

266
00:19:37,701 --> 00:19:40,555
in the world was pillaged beyond recognition.

267
00:19:40,556 --> 00:19:43,220
The Crusaders destroyed almost everything,

268
00:19:43,720 --> 00:19:45,249
including the Hippodrome.

269
00:19:45,250 --> 00:19:48,920
They looted four 5th century bronze horses on display in the

270
00:19:48,921 --> 00:19:50,942
Hippodrome and took them back to Venice.

271
00:19:50,943 --> 00:19:53,420
For centuries, they were on display at the famous

272
00:19:53,421 --> 00:19:55,653
Saint Mark's Cathedral.

273
00:19:55,654 --> 00:20:03,420
When the Ottomans conquered Istanbul in 1453, the great Hippodrome was in ruins.

274
00:20:03,421 --> 00:20:06,704
The Ottoman Empire also paid importance to this place.

275
00:20:06,705 --> 00:20:08,650
This was the center of the city.

276
00:20:08,651 --> 00:20:12,420
That's why they built all the beautiful buildings around here.

277
00:20:12,421 --> 00:20:22,420
Much of the elevated ground level we see today came about when dirt and debris from building the Blue Mosque was dumped to the side, right on top of the old race track.

278
00:20:22,421 --> 00:20:25,760
Construction of other Ottoman buildings in the square did the same.

279
00:20:26,800 --> 00:20:30,320
So beneath the ground of the square, I'd find the racetrack of the hippodrome.

280
00:20:30,920 --> 00:20:33,260
But just a few minutes' walk down a natural valley,

281
00:20:33,760 --> 00:20:37,460
Chemin was about to show me the only structural part of the hippodrome still standing.

282
00:20:37,860 --> 00:20:39,200
It's called the Svendome.

283
00:20:40,220 --> 00:20:41,640
You see, this is the Svendome.

284
00:20:41,940 --> 00:20:44,200
This is the outer wall of the hippodrome.

285
00:20:44,560 --> 00:20:49,200
It has a curve, and then you have a curve and a higher platform.

286
00:20:49,600 --> 00:20:50,480
We call it Svendome.

287
00:20:50,481 --> 00:20:58,480
Today, on top of the Svendome sits a more recent layer of the past, Sultanahmet Technical High School.

288
00:20:58,481 --> 00:21:02,480
This is a third century wall. It was part of the Hippodrome.

289
00:21:02,481 --> 00:21:06,480
And right now, its construction is so solid that it's holding up the school.

290
00:21:06,481 --> 00:21:08,480
Pretty good Roman engineering.

291
00:21:11,480 --> 00:21:15,480
The Svendome was holding up the high school and much of Sultanahmet Square.

292
00:21:15,481 --> 00:21:19,480
And it was all that was stopping me from getting beneath the ancient Hippodrome.

293
00:21:20,480 --> 00:21:29,680
The Blue Mosque is probably Istanbul's most famous landmark.

294
00:21:29,681 --> 00:21:35,860
It was built in the 17th century by some of the same stonemasons who helped construct the Taj Mahal in India.

295
00:21:36,440 --> 00:21:40,680
We now return to Cities of the Underworld on Modern Marvels.

296
00:21:43,820 --> 00:21:50,240
Most locals never think twice about the inconspicuous 3rd century wall in the middle of Istanbul's Sultanahmet Square.

297
00:21:50,480 --> 00:21:57,320
But beyond it lies the remains of the ancient Roman 100,000 seat Hippodrome.

298
00:21:57,321 --> 00:22:02,520
The area has never been fully excavated by archaeologists, but I was determined to see

299
00:22:02,521 --> 00:22:05,400
what remained of its underworld.

300
00:22:05,401 --> 00:22:09,700
Based on the Circus Maximus in Rome, the Hippodrome was built into a natural valley

301
00:22:09,701 --> 00:22:12,160
that slopes southward towards the sea.

302
00:22:12,161 --> 00:22:16,060
Engineers first closed off the south end of the valley with a U-shaped wall called the

303
00:22:16,061 --> 00:22:16,849
the Svendome.

304
00:22:16,850 --> 00:22:21,520
It rose over 130 feet high and linked up with the stands while the race track

305
00:22:21,521 --> 00:22:23,360
sat below on the valley floor.

306
00:22:23,361 --> 00:22:27,040
While the track was flat, the ground slopes down towards the

307
00:22:27,041 --> 00:22:31,940
Svendome, creating a cavernous underbelly or backstage, starting where the track ends

308
00:22:31,941 --> 00:22:34,026
and the Svendome begins.

